report: "# Report: endo-fs-must-cas-design\n\n## What was done\n\nThe design is committed on `job/endo-fs-must-cas-design/2` in\n`worktrees/endojs/endo-but-for-bots`, branched from `llm` at `036876777`:\n\n- `designs/fs-must-cas-attenuation.md` (new, 592 lines)\n- `designs/README.md` (summary table row, Milestone 10 row, dependency graph\n  node and two edges, per-design estimate row)\n\nOne commit: `135e09515 design(fs): must-compare-and-swap attenuation for\nfilesystem surfaces`. The steward pushes the branch and opens the pull\nrequest.\n\n## Pull request\n\nTitle: `design(fs): must-compare-and-swap attenuation for filesystem surfaces`\n\nDescription:\n\n> Adds `designs/fs-must-cas-attenuation.md`: given a filesystem capability,\n> make one of the same shape whose every mutator requires a precondition and\n> refuses a direct write. The precondition is one tagged record with five\n> forms (`absent`, `blob` hash, `tree` hash, name `binding`, `generation`),\n> passed as a trailing `{ expected, expectedTarget? }` option that exists on\n> the full surface and is required on the view. The compare lives in each\n> implementation under its own serialization; the view is a forwarding\n> wrapper in the shape of the extended `readOnly`. A third filesystem posture,\n> `compareAndSwap`, lets the 9P bridge, `attach`, `provideShell` and\n> `provideEnvironment` refuse to serve such a filesystem over a protocol with\n> no precondition slot.\n>\n> The core is an inventory of every state-changing method across\n> `@endo/platform` (lite and extended, three backends, layers, composers),\n> the daemon (mount, mount file, pet-name directory, host and guest, secrets,\n> content store, hashline), exo-git and git, the asset server, fs-exec,\n> spaces-util, the 9P server, agent tools, the CLI and the Rust crates. Each\n> row says where the method is defined, whether the attenuation applies, its\n> compare-and-swap form, or why it is refused or not applicable. Also:\n> per-backend atomicity, composition with `readOnly`, confinement, visible\n> roots, snapshots, trees and layers, errors, a test plan, a seven-step\n> build, and seven open questions. Reconciles with platform-fs,\n> fs-interface-consolidation and -reconciliation, endo-fs-backend-seam,\n> daemon-mount and -capabilities, daemon-capability-filesystem,\n> daemon-cas-management, filesystem-watchers, agent-tools-mount-fs-tools,\n> runtime-container-fs-mount, daemon-mount-visible-roots, cli-edit-verb,\n> exo-git-follow-root-advancement, endor-git-bindings and store-write-file.\n\n## Inventory size\n\n121 rows over 77 distinct surfaces (interfaces, modules, tools, commands and\ncrates). Many rows group the read methods of one interface, so the method\ncount is larger than the row count; every mutating method has its own row or\nshares a row only with its alias or its two-target twin.\n\n| Disposition | Rows |\n|---|---|\n| Applies, with a compare-and-swap form (`CAS`) | 41 |\n| Already compare-and-swap (`built`) | 6 |\n| Refused outright on the view (`refused`) | 7 |\n| Not applicable (`n/a`) | 67 |\n\nCounted with `awk` over the inventory tables' third column.\n\n## What I verified and how\n\n- Every symbol the design cites was found in the checkout by the four\n  inventory passes (platform, daemon, satellite packages and tools, Rust) and\n  spot-checked by me: `assertWritable` and `readOnlyView` in `mount.js`,\n  `applyEditPatch` and `validateEditPatch` in `hashline.js` (no caller in\n  `src/`), `posture.js`'s reader and writer kit, `readonly.js`'s `EACCES`\n  refusal, `makeLockTable` and `makeXattrsExo` in `wrap-backend.js`,\n  `effectiveReadOnly` in `manager.js`, `getMountBacking` in `host.js`, the\n  9P `T*` message set, and the Node `FilePowers` primitives.\n- Every relative link in the design resolves to a file in `designs/`.\n- All table rows have a consistent column count (one row had a `|` inside\n  backticks, fixed before the amend).\n- `garden prose diff llm` reports nothing.\n- The design has no mermaid fence; the README graph edit follows the\n  existing node and edge syntax but I did not run a mermaid parser.\n- Not run: `yarn lint`, `yarn docs`, tests. The change is markdown only\n  under `designs/`, which root Prettier does not format.\n\n## What I could not settle\n\nThe seven open questions in the design, in short: base64 only or both hash\nencodings; whether `ESTALE` carries the observed hash; persisting the stance\non mount formulas; a pet-store generation column versus the binding\nidentifier; an OS lock to close the external-writer window on node-fs and\nthe mount; threading `expected` through host and guest result names; and\nwhether a must-CAS `Git` facet should expose `applyPatch` and `update_ref_if`\nin place of refused porcelain.\n\nTwo conventions I chose on the maintainer's behalf:\n\n- The design is on the project's `llm` branch in the project's design\n  conventions (metadata table, not YAY front matter), as the job body asks,\n  although the garden's designer brief sends designs to the shed. The job\n  body is the maintainer's ask, so it won.\n- The author field reads `Kris Kowal (prompted)`, the neighbor convention.\n- The design is longer than the brief's one to three screens because the\n  job's core is an exhaustive inventory.\n\nI did not do a library lookup (`reference/library/`): the domain terms were\nall resolved against the project's own designs and code, which the job named.\nNo `outbox/library.yay`.\n\n## Lessons left\n\n- `skills/project-checkout/SKILL.md`: `garden project add` failed with\n  git's \"dubious ownership\" check on the garden's repository mirror; a\n  one-line `safe.directory` config outside the slot fixed it.\n\n## Build\n\nnext: build `fs-must-cas-attenuation` steps 1 and 2 (platform types, posture,\n`wrapBackend` compare, `mustCompareAndSwap` attenuator and conformance suite)\nagainst `llm` once the design lands; steps 3 to 7 follow as separate jobs.\nEstimate for the accountant: five to seven jobs, M-L in the README's scale,\n7 to 10 developer days; step 3 (daemon mount, directory, formula flag) is\nthe largest.\n"
